{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_2023_Iran%E2%80%93United_States_prisoner_release"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_2023_Iran%E2%80%93United_States_prisoner_release:\nIran–United States relations\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Map indicating locations of Iran and United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Iran_United_States_Locator_2.svg \"Map indicating locations of Iran and United States\") | |\n|  | |\n|  |  |\n| [Iran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran \"Iran\") | [United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") |\n| Diplomatic mission | |\n| *[Interests Section](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interests_Section_of_Iran_in_the_United_States \"Interests Section of Iran in the United States\") in the [Pakistani Embassy, Washington, D.C.](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_Pakistan,_Washington,_D.C. \"Embassy of Pakistan, Washington, D.C.\")* | *[Interests Section](about://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassy_of_the_United_States,_Tehran#U.S._Interests_Section_of_the_Swiss_Embassy \"Embassy of the United States, Tehran\") in the [Swiss Embassy](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_diplomatic_missions_of_Switzerland \"List of diplomatic missions of Switzerland\"), [Tehran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran \"Tehran\")* |\n| Envoy | |\n| Director of the Interest Section Mehdi Atefat | [United States Special Representative for Iran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ambassadors_of_the_United_States_to_Iran \"List of ambassadors of the United States to Iran\") Abram Paley |\n\n[![Head shot of Khamenei](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Ali_Khamenei_crop.jpg/120px-Ali_Khamenei_crop.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Khamenei_crop.jpg)\n\n[![Head shot of Joe Biden](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Joe_Biden_presidential_portrait_%28cropped%29.jpg/120px-Joe_Biden_presidential_portrait_%28cropped%29.jpg)](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Joe_Biden_presidential_portrait_(cropped).jpg)\n\nRelations between [Iran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran \"Iran\") and the [United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") during [Joe Biden](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden \"Joe Biden\")'s [term](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biden_administration \"Biden administration\") as the [president of the United States](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_the_United_States \"President of the United States\") (2021–2025) were shaped by efforts to revive the 2015 nuclear agreement alongside ongoing regional tensions, sanctions, cyberattacks, and proxy conflicts. Early in Biden's presidency, U.S. officials expressed interest in returning to the [Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_nuclear_deal \"Iran nuclear deal\") (JCPOA),[[1]](#cite_note-blunder-1) but negotiations in Vienna eventually stalled.[[2]](#cite_note-1953-2023_timeline-2)[[3]](#cite_note-stalling-3) [Iran increased uranium enrichment](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_program_of_Iran \"Nuclear program of Iran\") and imposed retaliatory sanctions,[[2]](#cite_note-1953-2023_timeline-2)[[4]](#cite_note-15sanct-4) while the U.S. imposed new sanctions over missile programs, oil exports, and human rights abuses.[[5]](#cite_note-Blinkdeal-5)[[6]](#cite_note-Moscsanct-6)[[7]](#cite_note-IranChina-7)\n\nTensions persisted throughout this era, marked by recurring proxy attacks on U.S. bases,[[8]](#cite_note-Kataib-8) which intensified following the outbreak of the Gaza war in late 2023,[[9]](#cite_note-proiran-9) and by subsequent American retaliatory strikes.[[10]](#cite_note-65milit-10) The period also saw disputes over the [assassination of Qasem Soleimani](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assassination_of_Qasem_Soleimani \"Assassination of Qasem Soleimani\"),[[11]](#cite_note-Motamedi-11)[[12]](#cite_note-avengeUS-12)[[13]](#cite_note-offercon-13) and military escalations across the Gulf region.[[14]](#cite_note-ErbilUS-14)[[15]](#cite_note-Iranseizes-15)[[16]](#cite_note-1000US-16) In 2023, a breakthrough occurred with a U.S.–Iran prisoner swap and the release of frozen Iranian funds,[[17]](#cite_note-6Bdeal-17)[[18]](#cite_note-humani-18)[[19]](#cite_note-breakth-19) though indirect diplomacy remained fragile.[[20]](#cite_note-EUhoped-20) Iran was later accused of interfering in the 2024 U.S. presidential election through cyber operations and AI disinformation.[[21]](#cite_note-Iranhack-21)[[22]](#cite_note-Iranmail-22)[[23]](#cite_note-AI3lines-23) Alleged assassination plots targeting Donald Trump and dissidents on U.S. soil further strained relations.[[24]](#cite_note-KD-24) By late 2024, relations remained adversarial, marked by unresolved security disputes and growing mistrust.\n\n## State of Iran-U.S. relations in January 2021\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Iran%E2%80%93United_States_relations_during_the_Biden_administration&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: State of Iran-U.S. relations in January 2021\")]\n\nAt the outset of the Biden administration in January 2021, Iran was led by President [Hassan Rouhani](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hassan_Rouhani \"Hassan Rouhani\"), a centrist cleric[[25]](#cite_note-25) who had previously championed the 2015 nuclear deal ([JCPOA](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JCPOA \"JCPOA\")) and advocated for improved relations with the West,[[26]](#cite_note-GuardianTimeline-26) while ultimate authority rested with Supreme Leader [Ali Khamenei](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei \"Ali Khamenei\"), who held decisive power over Iran’s foreign and security policies. Relations between the U.S. and Iran were deeply strained, with no formal diplomatic ties since 1980. The United States had [withdrawn](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_withdrawal_from_the_Iran_nuclear_deal \"United States withdrawal from the Iran nuclear deal\") from the [JCPOA](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_nuclear_deal \"Iran nuclear deal\"), reimposed sweeping sanctions, and escalated military tensions, while Iran had resumed higher-level uranium enrichment and reduced compliance with the nuclear deal in violation of the agreement.\n\n## Early diplomatic positions and regional tensions\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Iran%E2%80%93United_States_relations_during_the_Biden_administration&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Early diplomatic positions and regional tensions\")]\n\nThe Biden administration initially expressed interest in re-engaging with [Iran](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran \"Iran\") on the [Iran nuclear deal](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Comprehensive_Plan_of_Action \"Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action\"), which the previous administration had withdrawn from in 2018, sparking international backlash.[[27]](#cite_note-27)[[28]](#cite_note-28) Early in his presidency, Biden formally extended an offer to restart diplomatic talks on a new nuclear deal,[[29]](#cite_note-29) with Secretary of State [Antony Blinken](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antony_Blinken \"Antony Blinken\") indicated that the U.S. would consider re-entering the agreement if Iran returned to \"strict compliance\" with its terms.[[30]](#cite_note-30) At the time, Blinken also did not rule out the possibility of military intervention to prevent Iran from obtaining [nuclear weapons](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_states_with_nuclear_weapons \"List of states with nuclear weapons\").[[31]](#cite_note-31)[[32]](#cite_note-32)\n\nAs Iran continued to struggle with the [COVID-19 pandemic](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic \"COVID-19 pandemic\") in early 2021, the government repeatedly urged the [Biden administration](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biden_administration \"Biden administration\") to lift sanctions. Strict financial measures imposed by the previous [Trump administration](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump \"First presidency of Donald Trump\") had negatively impacted Iranian economies.[[33]](#cite_note-33)\n\nTensions remained high in the region. Following a [militia-organized rocket attack](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Erbil_rocket_attacks \"2021 Erbil rocket attacks\") in [Erbil](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erbil \"Erbil\"), Iraq, which wounded four American contractors and one soldier, in February 2021 the United States Military conducted airstrikes on Iran-backed militias in Syria, including [Kata'ib Hezbollah](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kata'ib_Hezbollah \"Kata'ib Hezbollah\").[[8]](#cite_note-Kataib-8) The attacks sparked renewed debate over presidential war powers, with key Democratic lawmakers expressing concern about conducting military operations without prior congressional approval.[[34]](#cite_note-34)\n\n## Nuclear negotiations begin\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Iran%E2%80%93United_States_relations_during_the_Biden_administration&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Nuclear negotiations begin\")]\n\nOver 220 US Congress leaders endorsed House Resolution 118 in April 2021, expressing support for \"the Iranian people's desire for a democratic, secular, and non-nuclear republic of Iran\" and condemning \"violations of human rights and state-sponsored terrorism\".[[35]](#cite_note-35) Around the same time, signatories of the [Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_nuclear_deal \"Iran nuclear deal\") (JCPOA) met in Vienna in an effort to bring both the United States and Iran back into compliance with the nuclear agreement. U.S. and Iranian officials participated in so-called [proximity talks](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximity_talks \"Proximity talks\") in order to exchange views on how to return to the considered deal. Both sides downplayed expectations for rapid progress, insisting that the other should resume their commitments first. Talks continued even after an explosion at Iran's [Natanz](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natanz \"Natanz\") nuclear facility, blamed on Israel, to which Iran responded by increasing uranium enrichment to 60% purity, its highest level to date.[[2]](#cite_note-1953-2023_timeline-2)\n\nDiplomatic tensions remained unresolved in late 2021, with U.S. Secretary of State Antony Blinken stating that military intervention to prevent Iran from obtaining [nuclear weapons](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_states_with_nuclear_weapons \"List of states with nuclear weapons\") could not be ruled out.[[36]](#cite_note-36)[[37]](#cite_note-37) Around the same time, Iran imposed san\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
