{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Mohammad_Mehdi_Karami"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Mohammad_Mehdi_Karami:\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\n| Mohammad Mehdi Karami | |\n| --- | --- |\n|  | |\n| Born | c. 1 November 2001 |\n| Died | 7 January 2023 (aged 21) |\n| Cause of death | [Execution by hanging](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_by_hanging \"Execution by hanging\") |\n| Known for | Third execution related to the [Mahsa Amini protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests \"Mahsa Amini protests\") |\n|  | |\n| Criminal charge | [Mofsed-e-filarz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mofsed-e-filarz \"Mofsed-e-filarz\") (\"corruption on Earth\") |\n| Penalty | [Death](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment \"Capital punishment\") |\n\n**Mohammad Mehdi Karami** ([Persian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language \"Persian language\"): محمدمهدی کرمی; c. 1 November 2001 – 7 January 2023) was a 21-year-old [Iranian-Kurdish](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurds_in_Iran \"Kurds in Iran\") man[[1]](#cite_note-:2-1)[[2]](#cite_note-:5-2) who was executed by the [Islamic Republic of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_of_Iran \"Islamic Republic of Iran\") for his involvement in the [Mahsa Amini protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests \"Mahsa Amini protests\"). He was convicted of *[Fisad-e-filarz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mofsed-e-filarz \"Mofsed-e-filarz\")* (an Arabic term translating to \"corruption on Earth\") for allegedly being involved in the killing of a [Basij](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij \"Basij\") militiaman during protests in [Karaj](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karaj \"Karaj\") commemorating the 40-day anniversary of [Hadis Najafi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadis_Najafi \"Hadis Najafi\")'s death. Karami was executed alongside 39-year-old volunteer children's coach [Seyyed Mohammad Hosseini](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Mohammad_Hosseini \"Execution of Mohammad Hosseini\"), another man who was also convicted of *Fisad-e-filarz* for his alleged involvement in the same killing.[[2]](#cite_note-:5-2) Both Karami and Hosseini asserted their innocence, and human rights organizations have accused Iranian authorities of using \"shoddy evidence\" to convict them.[[2]](#cite_note-:5-2)\n\n### Mahsa Amini protests\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Execution_of_Mohammad_Mehdi_Karami&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Mahsa Amini protests\")]\n\nThousands of protesters were detained as a result of the [Mahsa Amini protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests \"Mahsa Amini protests\"), and dozens were charged with offenses such as *[Moharebeh](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moharebeh \"Moharebeh\")* (\"waging war against God\") or *[Mofsed-e-filarz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mofsed-e-filarz \"Mofsed-e-filarz\")* (\"corruption on Earth\"), which are punishable by death in the [Islamic Republic of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_of_Iran \"Islamic Republic of Iran\").[[3]](#cite_note-:0-3) Prior to the executions of Karami and Hosseini, Iranian authorities executed [Mohsen Shekari](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohsen_Shekari \"Mohsen Shekari\") and [Majidreza Rahnavard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Majidreza_Rahnavard \"Execution of Majidreza Rahnavard\"), who were both convicted of *Moharebeh* due to separate alleged crimes connected to the Mahsa Amini protests.\n\nMost trials have taken place in Iran's [Islamic Revolutionary Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Court \"Islamic Revolutionary Court\") system. The system has received international criticism for holding trials behind closed doors and often not allowing defendants to review the evidence against them.[[4]](#cite_note-:33-4)[[5]](#cite_note-:62-5) In [Tehran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran \"Tehran\"), most trials have been presided over by Judge [Abolqasem Salavati](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abolqasem_Salavati \"Abolqasem Salavati\"), who faces U.S. sanctions for meting out harsh punishments. A statement by the [United States Department of the Treasury](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_the_Treasury \"United States Department of the Treasury\") criticized Salavati for handing down more than 100 death sentences and lengthy prison sentences to political prisoners, human rights activists, media workers, and \"others seeking to exercise freedom of assembly.\"[[6]](#cite_note-:20-6)\n\nAnalysts have warned that the Islamic Republic is \"determined to carry out mass executions,\" and \"keeps the execution cases as vague as possible to 'confuse' domestic and international reactions until the last minute.\" Hossein Bastani, a prominent political analyst for [BBC Persian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_Persian \"BBC Persian\"), said that \"worldwide, practical reactions might still prevent a human disaster.\"[[7]](#cite_note-:24-7) CNN independently confirmed in late December 2022 that there were 43 people under death sentences for crimes related to their involvement in the protests.[[1]](#cite_note-:2-1)\n\n### Mohammad Mehdi Karami's early life\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Execution_of_Mohammad_Mehdi_Karami&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Mohammad Mehdi Karami's early life\")]\n\nPrior to his execution, Karami was a [karate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate \"Karate\") champion. He had a tattoo of the [Olympic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympic_Games \"Olympic Games\") rings on his arm. Karami's cousin described him as \"a brave, intelligent boy\" who became interested in karate when he was 11 years old. He joined the Iranian youth national team and won at the national championships. In a video begging Iranian authorities not to execute his son, his father said Karami was \"the fourth ranked member on Iran's national team.\"[[1]](#cite_note-:2-1)[[8]](#cite_note-:3-8)\n\n## Allegations, arrest, and trial\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Execution_of_Mohammad_Mehdi_Karami&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: Allegations, arrest, and trial\")]\n\nIranian Judiciary spokesman Masoud Setayeshi was cited in a report alleging that a group of men, therein referred to as \"rioters,\" chased Ajamian with knives and stones, stripped him naked, and killed him during a demonstration to honor the 40-day anniversary of the [death of Hadis Najafi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_Hadis_Najafi \"Death of Hadis Najafi\"). The Iranian government frequently referred to protesters and anti-government demonstrators as \"rioters.\" On the day of the alleged crime, videos circulated on social media showing a prominent highway cordoned off with Ajamian's body, clad in a Basij uniform (despite the report claiming Ajamian had been stripped naked), lying lifeless on the ground. Iranian authorities detained sixteen people – thirteen adult men, including Mohammad Mehdi Karami and Seyyed Mohammad Hosseini, and three juvenile boys – in connection with Ajamian's killing. Setayeshi's report did not provide evidence to support the accusations that Karami and Hosseini were involved in the alleged crime.[[9]](#cite_note-:6-9)\n\nDuring his arrest, Karami said that he was beaten severely and subjected to \"severe physical and mental abuse by government agents,\" alleging that security forces beat him \"so hard [during his arrest], that he lost consciousness,\" leading arresting officers to believe he was dead; they then \"threw his body near a courthouse,\" but before they left, \"they realized he was still alive.\"[[10]](#cite_note-:4-10) Seyyed Mohammad Hosseini and his lawyer also accused authorities of using torture to extract his own confession; Hosseini's lawyer told [Iran International](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International \"Iran International\") that his confession was not legally valid because it was obtained \"under torture,\" and that Hosseini recited his confession while \"full of tears,\" alleging that authorities blindfolded him, bound his hands and feet, kicked him in the head \"until he fainted,\" struck the soles of his feet with an iron rod, and electrically shocked different parts of his body.[[10]](#cite_note-:4-10) [Amnesty International](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amnesty_International \"Amnesty International\") similarly alleged that Karami's confession was extracted through torture as well and that authorities relied on those forced confessions and \"shoddy evidence\" when convicting Karami and Hosseini.\n\nThe juveniles were tried in Iran's Criminal Courts, while Karami, Hosseini, and the other accused adults were tried in the [Islamic Revolutionary Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Court \"Islamic Revolutionary Court\"). Karami and Hosseini's trials, before Judge Moosa Asefolhosseini, lasted for less than one week. According to Karami's family and human rights groups, the two were denied the right to choose their own attorneys; the government appointed attorneys to represent them instead. At trial, both men denied the allegations made against them. On 5 December 2022, Karami and Hosseini were sentenced to death alongside three other men. The remaining eleven detainees, including the three juveniles, were sentenced to lengthy prison terms. The [Supreme Court of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Court_of_Iran \"Supreme Court of Iran\") accepted appeals from three demonstrators, claiming inadequate investigations, on 3 January 2023. However, the death sentences of Karami and Hosseini were upheld.[[2]](#cite_note-:5-2)[[9]](#cite_note-:6-9)[[11]](#cite_note-11)\n\n## Imprisonment and execution\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Execution_of_Mohammad_Mehdi_Karami&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Imprisonment and execution\")]\n\nWeeks before Karami's execution, his parents appeared in a video posted on social media begging Iranian authorities not to execute their son.[[1]](#cite_note-:2-1) His parents also alleged that Iranian authorities had [tortured](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torture \"Torture\") Karami during his time in detention. Karami's parents insisted that despite his mistreatment behind bars, he was in \"good spirits,\" but \"physically damaged\" f\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
