{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Mokhber"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Mokhber:\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\n| Mohammad Mokhber | |\n| --- | --- |\n| محمد مخبر | |\n| Mokhber in 2024 | |\n|  | |\n| Acting [President of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Iran \"President of Iran\") | |\n| **In office** 19 May 2024 – 28 July 2024 | |\n| [Supreme Leader](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Leader_of_Iran \"Supreme Leader of Iran\") | [Ali Khamenei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei \"Ali Khamenei\") |\n| [Vice President](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice_President_of_Iran \"Vice President of Iran\") | *Himself* |\n| Preceded by | [Ebrahim Raisi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebrahim_Raisi \"Ebrahim Raisi\") |\n| Succeeded by | [Masoud Pezeshkian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian \"Masoud Pezeshkian\") |\n| 7th [First Vice President of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice_presidents_of_Iran#First \"Vice presidents of Iran\") | |\n| **In office** 8 August 2021 – 28 July 2024 | |\n| President | * Ebrahim Raisi * *Himself* (acting) |\n| Preceded by | [Eshaq Jahangiri](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eshaq_Jahangiri \"Eshaq Jahangiri\") |\n| Succeeded by | [Mohammad Reza Aref](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Reza_Aref \"Mohammad Reza Aref\") |\n| Member of [Expediency Discernment Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expediency_Discernment_Council \"Expediency Discernment Council\") | |\n| **[Incumbent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incumbent \"Incumbent\")** | |\n| **Assumed office**  20 September 2022 | |\n| Appointed by | Ali Khamenei |\n| Chairman | [Sadeq Larijani](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sadeq_Larijani \"Sadeq Larijani\") |\n| Advisor and Aide to the [Supreme Leader of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Leader_of_Iran \"Supreme Leader of Iran\") | |\n| **[Incumbent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incumbent \"Incumbent\")** | |\n| **Assumed office**  23 September 2024 | |\n| Appointed by | [Ali Khamenei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei \"Ali Khamenei\") |\n| Head of the [Execution of Imam Khomeini's Order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Imam_Khomeini's_Order \"Execution of Imam Khomeini's Order\") | |\n| **In office** 15 July 2007 – 7 December 2021 | |\n| Appointed by | Ali Khamenei |\n| Preceded by | [Mohammad Javad Iravani](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Javad_Iravani \"Mohammad Javad Iravani\") |\n| Succeeded by | * [Aref Norouzi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aref_Norouzi?action=edit&redlink=1 \"Aref Norouzi (page does not exist)\") (acting) * [Parviz Fattah](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parviz_Fattah \"Parviz Fattah\") |\n| Personal details | |\n| Born | 26 June 1955 (age 71)  [Dezful](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dezful \"Dezful\"), Iran |\n| [Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_party \"Political party\") | [Independent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_politician \"Independent politician\") **Historical:** [Mojahedin of the Islamic Revolution Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mojahedin_of_the_Islamic_Revolution_Organization \"Mojahedin of the Islamic Revolution Organization\") |\n| Other political affiliations | [Principlist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_principlists \"Iranian principlists\") |\n| Children | 1 |\n| Signature |  |\n| Military service | |\n| Allegiance | [Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_of_Iran_Armed_Forces \"Islamic Republic of Iran Armed Forces\") |\n| Branch/service | [Revolutionary Guards](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps \"Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps\") |\n| Years of service | 1980-1988 |\n| Unit | Medical Corps |\n| Battles/wars | [Iran–Iraq War](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Iraq_War \"Iran–Iraq War\") |\n|  | |\n\n**Mohammad Mokhber**[[a]](#cite_note-1)[[b]](#cite_note-2) (born 26 June 1955)[[1]](#cite_note-birthday-3)[[2]](#cite_note-4) is an Iranian politician who served as the acting [president of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Iran \"President of Iran\") in 2024 following the death of President [Ebrahim Raisi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebrahim_Raisi \"Ebrahim Raisi\") in the [Varzaqan helicopter crash](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Varzaqan_helicopter_crash \"2024 Varzaqan helicopter crash\"), having been the [first vice president of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice_presidents_of_Iran#First \"Vice presidents of Iran\") from 2021 to 2024 under Raisi. He has been a member of the [Expediency Discernment Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expediency_Discernment_Council \"Expediency Discernment Council\") since September 2022 and serving as an advisor and aide to the [Supreme Leader of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Leader_of_Iran \"Supreme Leader of Iran\") since September 2024.[[3]](#cite_note-auto-5)\n\nPreviously, he was also the head of the [Execution of Imam Khomeini's Order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Execution_of_Imam_Khomeini's_Order \"Execution of Imam Khomeini's Order\") committee (EIKO, a.k.a. Setad), an executive at the [Mostazafan Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mostazafan_Foundation \"Mostazafan Foundation\"), chairman of the board of [Sina Bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sina_Bank \"Sina Bank\"), deputy governor of [Khuzestan Province](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khuzestan_Province \"Khuzestan Province\") and CEO of Dezful Telecommunications. During the [Iraq-Iran war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iraq-Iran_war \"Iraq-Iran war\"), he was a medical officer in the [Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps \"Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps\") (IRGC), and before the war he was a member of the revolutionary Mansurun Group.[[4]](#cite_note-6)\n\nMokhber's career from young revolutionary to public official is dogged with allegations of corruption, some of which have led to investigations, regarding privatization of Iran's public assets, insider trading, human rights abuses, and affiliation with Iran's nuclear program. These resulted in international sanctions of Mokhber and his affiliate organizations, Sina Bank and Mostazafan. Mokhber is subject to secondary sanctions which limit his global travel and financial liquidity.[[5]](#cite_note-7)\n\nUpon Raisi's death, and with the approval of the [Supreme Leader of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Leader_of_Iran \"Supreme Leader of Iran\"), Mokhber assumed \"the powers and functions of the President\" as the then-incumbent first vice president. In accordance with the [Constitution of Iran](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_of_Iran \"Constitution of Iran\"), a [Temporary Presidential Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporary_Presidential_Council \"Temporary Presidential Council\") consisting of the Acting President, the Speaker of the Assembly, and the Head of Judiciary was formed and a [new election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Iranian_presidential_election \"2024 Iranian presidential election\") date was set for June 28, 2024 with a run-off date of July 5, 2024.[[6]](#cite_note-8) [Masoud Pezeshkian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian \"Masoud Pezeshkian\") won the presidential runoff election in July 2024,[[7]](#cite_note-:0-9) and was appointed as the President on 28 July 2024.[[8]](#cite_note-:1-10)\n\n## Early life and education\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Mohammad_Mokhber&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: Early life and education\")]\n\nMohammad Mokhber was born in [Dezful](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dezful \"Dezful\"),[[9]](#cite_note-1stVP-11) Iran, on 26 June 1955 to a clerical family.[[10]](#cite_note-12) He possesses two doctoral degrees, including a doctorate academic paper (and an MA) in international law; he reportedly has a doctorate degree in management and economic development planning.[[11]](#cite_note-Mokhber_bio-13) He also holds an additional MA in management and a bachelor's degree in electrical engineering.[[12]](#cite_note-14)[[13]](#cite_note-15)[[14]](#cite_note-16)\n\nAs a teenager, Mokhber helped lay the groundwork for the Iranian Revolution in his home city of Dezful by joining the Mansurun Group, a guerrilla organization which worked to combat the [Pahlavi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pahlavi_dynasty \"Pahlavi dynasty\") regime. Through Mansurun, he collaborated with prominent figures in the IRGC and current conservative politicians including Ali and Mohammad Jahanara, Ali Shamkhani, Mohsen Rezaei, Mohammad Forouzandeh, Mohammad Baqer Zulqader and Seyed Ahmed Avai.[[15]](#cite_note-17) During the Iran-Iraq war, Mokhber became a medical officer with the IRGC in his home town of Dezful. In the 1990s, Mokhber was the CEO of Dezful Telecommunications, then deputy governor of [Khuzestan Province](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khuzestan_Province \"Khuzestan Province\").[[16]](#cite_note-UANI-18)[[17]](#cite_note-ajwho-19)[[18]](#cite_note-independentpersian.com-20)\n\nIn the early 2000s Mokhber's influence expanded considerably when he became Vice President of [Mostazafan Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mostazafan_Foundation \"Mostazafan Foundation\"), the second largest commercial enterprise in Iran behind the state-owned National Iranian Oil Company and biggest holding company in the Middle East.[[19]](#cite_note-21) He was additionally made CEO of the foundation's Trade and Transport organization[[20]](#cite_note-bbc.com-22) and placed on the board of two entities apparently linked to the foundation: Sina Bank, 84% of which is owned by Mostazafan,[[21]](#cite_note-opensanctions.org-23) and MTN Irancell. While Iranian corporate records are not available to substantiate MTN Irancell's true ownership, a lawsuit filed by Turkcell in 2004 alleges 51% of the company is owned by Mostazafan.[[22]](#cite_note-ReferenceA-24)[[23]](#cite_note-25) Both Sina Bank and MTN Irancell were involved in international scandals concerning activities that took place during Mokhber's tenure, which contributed to sanctions of Mokhber, Sina Bank and Mostazafan (see Corruption and Abuse of Power, and Sanctions).\n\nAs chairperson of Sina Bank's board, Mokh\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
